## Summary
This article reports allegations that COVID-19 data was manipulated in Liguria, Italy, according to public prosecutors. Governor Giovanni Toti is implicated, admitting to inflating contagion numbers in discussions with his chief of staff, Matteo Cozzani. The scandal raises concerns about data integrity and its impact on public health policies.

## Key Points
- Public prosecutors in Liguria allege systematic manipulation of COVID-19 pandemic data.
- Governor Giovanni Toti admitted to increasing contagion figures in conversations with his chief of staff.
- The manipulation involved direct communication between Toti and Matteo Cozzani.
- This incident highlights potential misconduct and data falsification in administrative handling of the pandemic.
- The allegations could affect trust in public health decisions and research based on such data.


